        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Names",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Labrum, mandibles, maxillae, labium, hypopharynx",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "The labrum is the upper lip and the labium",
          "is the lower lip.",
          "Each mandible is a hard toothed jaw that cuts",
          "and grinds the food.",
          "Each maxilla has a cardo and a stipes, and",
          "carries the galea, the lacinia and a",
          "maxillary palp.",
          "The labium has a submentum, a mentum and a",
          "prementum, and carries the glossa, the",
          "paraglossa and a labial palp.",
          "The hypopharynx is the median tongue. The",
          "salivary duct opens at its base."
        ],
        "why": "The small pieces are easier to remember as two matching sets: the maxilla and the labium are built the same way, each with a base, two lobes and a palp.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Mixing up galea and lacinia with glossa and paraglossa. The first pair belongs to the maxilla, the second to the labium.",
          "Writing that the mandibles carry palps. Only the maxillae and the labium carry palps."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Maxilla and labium match: base, two lobes, one palp each. Galea and lacinia on the maxilla, glossa and paraglossa on the labium.",
        "margin_note": "One line per piece, in the same order as the drawing. Do not repeat the labels already on the figure at length."
      }
    ]
  },
  "insider_note": "This is a figure question, so the credit sits almost entirely in the labels — the pieces most often missed are the hypopharynx and the two palps, and an unlabelled neat drawing scores close to nothing."
